      "slitSeparation": 1
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.62
}

NOTES: Render wavefront arcs as THREE.Line / LineSegments built from EllipseCurve points in the XY plane (fronts are drawn as 2D arcs in the vertical plane containing the beam, matching the source figure's flat arc rendering) — do not make them 3D spherical shells. Use additive blending or plain lines with depthWrite false so overlapping arc families read as the crosshatched interference region. Detection screens: draw the fringe pattern into a CanvasTexture (1 x 512 gradient stretched across Y, or full 256x512 canvas) recomputed whenever slitSeparation changes; dots on panel (b) screens are best as a THREE.Points cloud with a circular sprite texture, its draw range animated by the timeLapse animation (drawRange from 30 to 1400) rather than regenerating geometry. Reuse one precomputed inverse-CDF sampler of I(y) so that changing the slider re-samples the same random uniforms and dots visibly migrate into the new band positions instead of reshuffling. Panels are mutually exclusive via modelToggle: hide the inactive panel's group entirely, keeping camera framing fixed so the shared barrier stays in place and the comparison is legible. Slits are cut from the gray barrier with a simple three-quad construction (above, between, below) rather than CSG, and rendered double-sided with flat MeshBasicMaterial to match the flat-shaded textbook look. Labels via CSS2DRenderer so text stays screen-aligned and crisp under orbit.

