CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure contains two panels, (a) and (b), that together demonstrate the wave–particle duality of electrons using a double-slit experiment. Panel (a) presents the wave picture: an electron source on the left sends waves — depicted as expanding concentric golden arcs — through a barrier containing two narrow slits, after which the wavefronts from each slit overlap and interfere to produce a classic banded interference pattern of alternating bright and dark fringes on the black detection screen at the right. Panel (b) presents the particle picture evolving over time: electrons (shown as individual yellow dots) leave the source as discrete particles, pass through the two slits in the barrier, and are recorded one by one on three successive detection screens arranged along a rightward "Time" axis; the first screen shows only a sparse, seemingly random scatter of hits, the second shows more dots still without an obvious pattern, and the third reveals the same banded interference pattern seen in panel (a) fully resolved from the accumulated detections. This figure motivates the concept of wave–particle duality by demonstrating that electrons exhibit clear particle-like behavior when detected individually yet collectively produce the wavelike interference pattern that Davisson and Germer observed experimentally, establishing the quantum mechanical reality that both descriptions are necessary.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Panel (a): electron source at far left (implied point/line source) with 'Electron source' text label
  - Panel (a): family of expanding concentric golden circular wavefront arcs travelling right from the source toward the barrier
  - Panel (a): opaque gray barrier plane standing in the beam path, drawn as a tilted parallelogram, with two thin horizontal white slits separated vertically about its center
  - Panel (a): two sets of secondary golden circular wavefronts emanating from each slit on the downstream side
  - Panel (a): overlapping/crossing wavefront region between the slits and the screen, forming a widening cone of interference toward the right
  - Panel (a): black detection screen parallel to the barrier at the right end, showing horizontal alternating bright-gold and dark interference fringes
  - Panel (a): caption label '(a)'
  - Panel (b): 'Electron source' text label with two thick gold block arrows pointing right into the barrier
  - Panel (b): identical gray barrier parallelogram with two horizontal white slits
  - Panel (b): scattered individual gold dots upstream of the barrier and a sparser spray of dots downstream travelling toward the screens
  - Panel (b): three black detection screens at increasing x, each a tilted parallelogram, with increasing dot counts (sparse random scatter, moderate scatter, dense banded pattern)
  - Panel (b): horizontal black 'Time' arrow beneath the three screens with bold 'Time' label
  - Panel (b): caption label '(b)'

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
Beam axis is +X (screen-right). Barrier and all detection screens are planes with normal along X, spanning Y (vertical, the direction of slit separation and fringe variation) and Z (receding depth). Panel (a) layout: source point at x=-7.5, y=0, z=0; gray barrier plane at x=-4.0 sized 5.0 (Y) x 4.0 (Z), color #8C8C8C, drawn with its top edge rising toward screen-right in the axonometric pose; two white slits are thin horizontal lines (full Z extent, 0.08 in Y) at y=+d/2 and y=-d/2 with d = slitSeparation (default 1.0). Detection screen at x=+5.0, same 5.0 x 4.0 size, near-black #0D0D0D. Upstream wavefronts: ~22 concentric golden arcs (color #E5A823, line width ~2px) centered on the source, radii 1.0 to 3.6 in steps of 0.12, drawn only in the forward hemisphere. Downstream: two families of ~30 arcs centered on each slit, radii 0.1 to 9.0 step 0.12, clipped to the cone between barrier and screen so the overlap region widens from ~0.6 tall at the barrier to the full screen height. Screen fringe intensity I(y) = cos^2(pi*d*y/(lambda*L)) * sinc-like envelope, with lambda = 0.22 world units and L = 9.0 (barrier-to-screen distance); default d = 1.0 gives ~2.0-unit fringe spacing, i.e. about 6-7 bright horizontal bands across the 5-unit screen. Bright bands are pale gold (#E8C06A to #FFF0C0 at peak), dark bands #0D0D0D. Panel (b) layout: same source/barrier positions; two thick gold block arrows (#E0A500, ~0.9 long, 0.35 thick) at y=+0.7 and y=-0.7, x from -6.8 to -5.6, pointing +X. Dots are gold discs (#E8A80C) radius ~0.05, ~80 scattered upstream in x in [-6.5,-4.0], ~40 sparse downstream in x in [-3.8, 2.0]. Three black screens at x = 3.0, 5.6, 8.2, each 4.2 (Y) x 3.4 (Z), each offset slightly so they read as a receding row; dot counts 30, 220, 1400 respectively, with y sampled from the probability density I(y) above (uniform in z) — the 30-dot screen looks random, the 1400-dot screen reproduces the panel-(a) bands exactly. Horizontal black arrow from x=2.6 to x=9.0 at y=-3.0 below the screens with bold 'Time' label centered under it. Text labels: 'Electron source' in black sans-serif to the left of the barrier at y~0; panel captions '(a)' and '(b)' centered below each panel.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "modelToggle",
    "type": "toggle",
    "teaches": "The same experiment supports two descriptions: continuous waves interfering, and discrete particles landing one at a time that only build the same fringes after many detections.",
